<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adam Smith</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1723">1723</a>–<a href="/years/1790">1790</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adam Smith was a Scottish philosopher and economist of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-enlightenment">Scottish Enlightenment</a>. After The Theory of Moral Sentiments of 1759 he wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-wealth-of-nations">An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations</a>, published in 1776, which argued that the division of labour and free markets guided by self-interest produce prosperity. It became the founding work of modern economics, and he is often called its father.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adam-smith.jpg" alt="Adam Smith" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adam_Smith_The_Muir_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
